+.. _intel-hda-ace:
+
+Intel ACE 1.0 audio DSP (``intel-hda-ace``)
+============================================
+
+``intel-hda-ace`` is a model of the Intel Smart Sound Technology BUS
+function of Arrow Lake-S/HX (PCI ``8086:7f50``, class 0403, programming
+interface 0x80): the Intel HD Audio controller of ``intel-hda`` (BAR0, with
+its codec bus and, on this variant, the extended-capability chain) plus the
+ACE 1.0 audio DSP behind the function's BAR4.  It exists so that an operating
+system's Intel SST driver can be run and observed in a VM without the
+hardware: the DSP's power-up, the ROM boot handshake, the code-loader stream
+that carries the firmware, the IPC4 receive/reply path and the
+controller-level interrupt are modelled as a state machine driven by
+virtual-clock timers.
+
+It is a *model*, not a DSP: the "firmware" does not execute, the image the
+host streams is only judged (its container markers and the segments its
+module table loads), and every value the hardware ABI does not dictate is a
+named property listed below.
+
+Usage
+-----
+
+::
+
+   -audiodev none,id=snd0 -device intel-hda-ace,bus=pcie.0,addr=1b.0,id=ace \
+   -device hda-duplex,audiodev=snd0
+
+The codec attaches to the controller's HDA bus by itself.  ``1b.0`` is the
+PCH address the real function uses on Intel platforms; a guest PCI
+enumerator does not depend on it.
+
+What the guest sees
+-------------------
+
+- BAR0: the ICH6 register block (0x4000 bytes) with the extended-capability
+  chain the ACE-era controllers carry: ``LLCH`` (0x14) reads 0x180, the
+  processing-power capability (id 3: ``PPCTL``, ``PPSTS``, ``PPCH``) at
+  0x180, and the software-position-in-buffer capability (id 4: ``SPBFCCTL``
+  and one ``SPIB``/``MAXFIFO`` pair per engine) at 0x700.
+- BAR4 (2 MB by default): the ACE 1.0 register block (``ADSPCS``, the
+  core power control, the interrupt pointer and enables, the IPC
+  registers, the firmware status and ROM error registers), SRAM window 0
+  at 0x180000 (the firmware register block at +0, the uplink mailbox at
+  +0x1000) and the downlink window at 0x188000.
+
+The boot handshake as modelled: the subsystem and the core come up with a
+delay after their power-active requests (CPA), the purge request is
+acknowledged with DONE and the ROM reaches INIT_DONE, the code-loader
+stream (the output stream whose tag the purge request named) is consumed
+through DMA up to its SPIB value, the ROM walks its load states and enters
+the firmware, the firmware register block appears in window 0 and a
+FW_READY notification is posted; the FW_CONFIG request is answered with a
+TLV list whose version is read from the streamed image.  The interrupt
+(INTx or MSI) is asserted only while a notification or reply is pending and
+the host has set every enable (the register the interrupt pointer names,
+the host IPC enable, the IPC block's busy/done enables) and the
+controller's INTCTL gates it like any other source (INTSTS bit 30).
+
+Properties
+----------
+
+``personality``
+   The ACE 1.0 controller to present: ``arl-s`` (default, ``8086:7f50``),
+   ``mtl`` (``7e28``), ``arl`` (``7728``), ``lnl-p`` (``a828``), ``ptl``
+   (``e428``), ``ptl-h`` (``e328``).  All share the ACE 1.0 register map.
+``x-bar4-size``
+   Size of BAR4 (default 0x200000; the Linux and LiberNT drivers need at
+   least 0x189000).  Must be a power of two.
+``x-powered-down-value``
+   What the DSP-domain registers and SRAM windows read while the DSP is
+   powered down (default 0; the real value is unknown, INFO_NEEDED of the
+   plan).
+``x-delay-scale``
+   Multiplies every modelled delay (default 1.0): with 1.5 the firmware
+   entry takes 3 ms instead of 2, which sits at the edge of the Linux
+   loader's 3 s budget only when scaled further.
+``fault``
+   The fault injector, settable at run time through QMP (``qom-set
+   /machine/peripheral/ace fault <name>``): ``none`` (default),
+   ``never-power``, ``never-ack``, ``never-ready``, ``never-enter``,
+   ``never-fwready``, ``never-reply``, ``drop-requests``,
+   ``halt-rom=<code>``, ``halt-fw=<code>``, ``crash-notification``,
+   ``abi=<n>``, ``ipc-status=<n>``, ``bad-tuples``, ``truncated-reply``,
+   ``version-shift``, ``window-read-fail``.
+``msi``, ``debug``, ``old_msi_addr``
+   As for ``intel-hda``.  ``debug=2`` traces the controller's register
+   accesses.
+
+Model values (not measured on hardware)
+--------------------------------------
+
+BAR4 2 MB, the capability offsets 0x180/0x700, the interrupt pointer
+``HFINTIPPTR`` = 0x120 (the interrupt-enable register sits at BAR4 + 0x120),
+powered-down reads 0, the timings (CPA 100/200 us after the requests, DONE
+100 us after the purge, INIT_DONE 500 us later, firmware entry 2 ms after
+the stream runs, FW_READY 200 us later, a reply 50 us after a request), the
+ROM info word 0x0120315A and the firmware configuration constants of the
+reply (4096/4096-byte mailboxes, 128 KiB trace log, 16 pipelines, 4
+libraries).  Traces print them as "(model value)".
+
+Tracing
+-------
+
+``-trace 'ace_*'`` (BAR4 accesses, the ROM walk, the stream consumption,
+the payload rules, the IPC messages, the interrupt, the state changes) and
+``-trace 'intel_hda_cap_*'`` (the BAR0 capability registers).  Any BAR4
+offset outside the model reads the powered-down value and is traced as
+``ace_unexpected``, which lists every register a guest touches that the
+model lacks.
+
+Limits
+------
+
+Not modelled: the DSP's execution, signature verification (the host never
+verifies; the ROM's rules are choices), SoundWire, the ML capability, real
+DMA timing, D0i3 and wake, and anything the silicon does that no emulator
+can show (a hang on a bad power sequence, the true ROM checks and timings).
